+**Calls back.** `reentrant` before an extern's `fn` (`extern reentrant fn
+walk(t: Tree);`, or `reentrant fn walk(t: Tree);` in a block) says its C may
+call the program's export fns back while it runs. Each caller, and theirs,
+publishes every cached data stack top before the call, so a call back
+starts above all live data, the elements of a builder the extern was
+handed included, and leaves the stacks as it found them: C appending to
+such a builder after a call back appends where it would have. C finds an
+export fn through the runtime's hooks (below), by its C symbol and its
+signature as Goose writes it (`i64,const u8[:]->bool`), and only on the main
+program's thread, whose stacks the export wrappers use. Only an export fn
+that touches no `var` global, itself or through its callees, can be called
+back: the code a call back interrupts assumed the extern left the globals
+alone, and may hold references into them.
+
+A library that exports `void goose_runtime_hooks(const gs_rt_hooks *)` is
+handed the runtime's hooks as it is opened (`goose.h` declares the struct):
+`abort_msg(msg, len, file, line)`, which ends the program exactly as
+`abort(msg)` does, or only the worker in a contained one (§11.2), and
+`at_end(fn)`, which has the runtime call `fn` when the program ends, by a
+return from `main` or by `exit`; and, from version 2, `reentry_lookup(name,
+sig, &why)`, the wrapper of an export fn a call back may reach, and
+`on_program_thread()`. A library never sees a hook it did not ask for.
+
 A library is never closed. A package's `main_thread_only` restriction
 (docs/packages.md) applies to the members of its blocks as it does to its
 other externs. A variadic C function cannot be bound: the declaration has
